  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nFor Sunday, dry weather will continue. Temperatures will run \nright near normal for late-September, with lowland highs in the \nmid-80s. Plenty of high cloud coverage will be a precursor to the \nstrong moisture advection coming our way.\n\nTwo weather features to focus on in the short term are the upper \nlow currently centered off the Oregon coast to our north, and \nHurricane Polo spinning over the eastern Pacific to our south. \nTropical steering pattern is favorable for Polo to drift north \ntoward Baja California next few days, eventually latching onto the\nstronger mid-latitude jet flow next week and dissipating as it \nmoves northeast into Sonora/Chihuahua.\n\nMonday will feature a rapid moisture increase locally, thanks to \nthe approaching tropical remnants. Pacific low aloft will make a \nsoutheast dive down to the Colorado River Valley on Monday, forced\nby a strengthening 75-knot jet streak on its backside. Veering of\nmid-level winds to the south ahead of this system will pull in \ndeep, tropically-sourced moisture into southern New Mexico and far\nwest Texas. Precipitable water values, measured at just 0.7\" this\nevening (00Z KEPZ RAOB), will spike to 1.5-1.6\" Monday evening. \nThis may break seasonal moisture records for El Paso, and is \neasily 3 to 4 standard deviations higher than usual for the end of\nthe monsoon season. This moisture-rich atmosphere, combined with \nPVA and increasing diffluent flow aloft, will lead to widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms area wide starting as early as Monday \nafternoon.\n\nGood confidence in rain for all local zones Monday night through \nWednesday morning as the upper low pulls up tropical moisture like\na fire hose into New Mexico and West Texas. 48-hour rainfall \nforecasts suggest widespread totals ranging from 1.00-2.00\". NBM \nguidance backs this up. Stronger storms and training (or multiple \nrounds) of rain showers over the same areas will lead to localized\n3-4\". Soil saturation is very high right now, so rain will lead \nto rapid runoff and flash flooding quickly. A Moderate (Level 3 of\n4) Risk of flash flooding is in place almost area-wide for \nTuesday. Of particular concern for me will be the Sacramento \nMountains, which have already had an above-normal precipitation \nseason and significant flooding over the past two weeks. Current \nplan is to issue a Flood Watch, collaborated with neighboring \nWFOs, on Sunday in advance of this event. WFO El Paso will be \nliberal with flood warnings in areas with recent heavy rains if \nneeded Tuesday-Wednesday.\n\nBoth GFS and ECMWF global models show a track of the upper low \nprogressing across central NM on Wednesday. Rain showers will \nslowly decrease in coverage Wednesday morning, likely becoming dry\nby Wednesday evening. \n\nOnce all this action passes, it looks like a fairly \"boring\" \nweather pattern for the start of October. Strengthening upper high\nover the U.S. Southwest will usher in a much-needed break from \nrainfall. Temperatures will be cool for this time of year, with \nlowland highs lingering in the upper 70s the rest of the week \nahead.",
